rs in the same time period, and the output carries the time dimension.\",\"type\":\"Selection\",\"options\":[\"Spatial\",\"Spatio-temporal\"],\"default\":\"Spatial\",\"mode\":\"toggle\",\"value\":\"Spatial\"},{\"name\":\"indexcol\",\"title\":\"Index column\",\"description\":\"The H3 or Quadbin index column identifying each cell (the grid type is detected automatically). Neighbors are the cell's k-ring on this grid.\",\"type\":\"Column\",\"parent\":\"embeddings_table\",\"dataType\":[\"number\",\"string\"],\"value\":\"GEOID\"},{\"name\":\"date_column\",\"title\":\"Date column\",\"description\":\"The column holding the time period of each cell. Neighbors are matched within the same period and the date is carried through to the output.\",\"type\":\"Column\",\"parent\":\"embeddings_table\",\"dataType\":[\"number\",\"date\",\"datetime\",\"time\",\"timestamp\"],\"showIf\":[{\"parameter\":\"analysis_type\",\"value\":\"Spatio-temporal\"}],\"value\":\"distance\"},{\"name\":\"size\",\"title\":\"Size\",\"description\":\"How many rings of neighbors around each cell to include. One ring is the six or eight cells touching it, and each extra ring widens the neighborhood and smooths the result. Every kernel covers the same rings, so the kernel changes how much each ring counts and never which cells take part.\",\"type\":\"Number\",\"default\":1,\"min\":1,\"max\":10,\"value\":1},{\"name\":\"kernel\",\"title\":\"Kernel function for spatial weights\",\"description\":\"How to weight neighbors by their ring distance. 'uniform' weights every ring equally. 'triangular', 'quadratic', 'quartic' and 'gaussian' give nearer rings more weight. Every kernel includes the same rings, and the weights divide out, so only the relative weighting matters.\",\"type\":\"Selection\",\"options\":[\"uniform\",\"triangular\",\"quadratic\",\"quartic\",\"gaussian\"],\"default\":\"uniform\",\"value\":\"uniform\"},{\"name\":\"metric\",\"title\":\"Distance metric\",\"description\":\"The function used to compare vectors. Cosine normalizes the vectors itself and is correct whatever their length. Dot product is the same comparison without that normalization: faster, but valid only if every embedding already has length 1. If you do not know the magnitude of your embeddings, choose Cosine.\",\"type\":\"Selection\",\"options\":[\"Cosine\",\"Dot product\"],\"default\":\"Cosine\",\"value\":\"Dot product\"}],\"version\":\"1.0.0\"},\"position\":{\"x\":1168,\"y\":224},\"zIndex\":2},{\"id\":\"12588cb1-9da1-4824-92fb-50d741cdbb7f\",\"type\":\"generic\",\"data\":{\"type\":\"generic\",\"label\":\"Visualization\",\"name\":\"visualization\",\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"input_table\",\"title\":\"Embedding table\",\"description\":\"The table containing the embeddings to visualize\",\"type\":\"Table\"},{\"name\":\"analysis_type\",\"title\":\"Analysis type\",\"description\":\"Determines whether the analysis is spatial or spatio-temporal\",\"type\":\"Selection\",\"options\":[\"Spatial\",\"Spatio-temporal\"],\"default\":\"Spatial\",\"mode\":\"toggle\",\"value\":\"Spatial\"},{\"name\":\"geoid\",\"title\":\"ID column\",\"description\":\"The column containing the IDs of the embeddings\",\"type\":\"Column\",\"parent\":\"input_table\",\"dataType\":[\"number\",\"string\"],\"value\":\"GEOID\"},{\"name\":\"date\",\"title\":\"Date column\",\"description\":\"The column containing the dates of the embeddings\",\"type\":\"Column\",\"parent\":\"input_table\",\"dataType\":[\"number\",\"date\",\"datetime\",\"time\",\"timestamp\"],\"showIf\":[{\"parameter\":\"analysis_type\",\"value\":\"Spatio-temporal\"}],\"value\":\"r_channel\"},{\"name\":\"reduction_mode\",\"title\":\"Dimensionality reduction technique\",\"description\":\"The technique to use to reduce the dimensionality of the embeddings\",\"type\":\"Selection\",\"options\":[\"PCA\",\"Manual\"],\"default\":\"PCA\",\"value\":\"PCA\"},{\"name\":\"color_mapping\",\"title\":\"Color mapping\",\"description\":\"How the reduced components are mapped to colors: 'RGB' maps